UnicodeMathML: a function argument whose atoms are not a list no longer throws

`sin qa_n`, `lim_(n→∞) qa_n` (a function applied to two letters, the
second subscripted) threw "Cannot read properties of undefined (reading
'chars')" in preprocess, and the whole equation came back as an error
span: the check for the "⒡" (parenthesize argument) dictation option read
`x.atoms[0].chars`, but `atoms` is a single object there, not an array.
Found by MateX's navigation audit: typing a letter at the start of a
limit's argument dropped the built equation.

Only dist/unicodemathml.js, the file MateX loads. Applied by
scripts/bootstrap.sh after 0001 and 0002.

--- a/dist/unicodemathml.js
+++ b/dist/unicodemathml.js
@@ -3450,13 +3450,16 @@
                 if (Array.isArray(x))
                     x = x[0];                  // '⒡' as separate array element
                 if (x != undefined && x.hasOwnProperty('atoms')) {
-                    let ch = x.atoms[0].chars;
-                    if (ch[0] == '⒡') {
+                    // MateX: atoms is not always an array ("sin qa_n"): x.atoms[0]
+                    // was undefined there and the whole conversion threw.
+                    let first = Array.isArray(x.atoms) ? x.atoms[0] : x.atoms;
+                    let ch = first && first.chars;
+                    if (typeof ch == 'string' && ch[0] == '⒡') {
                         // Remove '⒡' and enclose function arg in parens
                         if (ch.length == 1)
                             ofFunc[0].shift();
                         else
-                            ofFunc[0].atoms[0].chars = ch.substring(1);
+                            first.chars = ch.substring(1);
                         ofFunc = {bracketed: {open: '(', close: ')', content: ofFunc}}
                     }
                 }
